Git Command Categories
Category	Commands
Repository	git init, git clone
Status	git status, git diff
Staging	git add, git restore --staged
Commit	git commit, git log, git show
File Management	git rm, git mv, git restore, git clean
Remote	git remote, git fetch, git pull, git push
1. git init

Creates a new Git repository.

mkdir demo
cd demo
git init

Output:

Initialized empty Git repository

Git creates a hidden .git directory.

Verify:

ls -la

You'll see:

.git
2. git clone

Copies an existing remote repository.

git clone https://github.com/user/project.git

or

git clone git@github.com:user/project.git

Creates:

project/
├── .git
├── README.md
└── src/
3. git status ⭐

The most frequently used command.

git status

Shows:

Current branch
Modified files
Staged files
Untracked files

Example:

On branch main

Changes not staged:
README.md

Untracked files:
notes.txt

Use it before almost every Git operation.

4. git add

Moves changes to the staging area.

Single file:

git add app.py

Multiple files:

git add app.py README.md

Everything:

git add .
5. git commit

Creates a snapshot.

git commit -m "Added login feature"

Best practices:

✅ Small commits

✅ Clear messages

Examples:

Fix login bug

Add payment API

Update Terraform module

Avoid:

Changes

Update

Fixed stuff
6. git log

Shows commit history.

git log

Compact:

git log --oneline

Graph:

git log --graph --oneline

Example:

* a123456 Added login
* b987654 Initial commit
7. git show

Displays detailed information about a commit.

git show

Specific commit:

git show a123456

Shows:

Commit message
Author
Date
File changes
8. git diff

Shows changes before committing.

Example:

print("Hello")

Modified:

print("Hello Azure")

Run:

git diff

Git highlights the exact changes.

Compare staged changes
git diff --staged

Useful before committing.

9. git rm

Removes a file and stages the deletion.

git rm old.txt

Commit:

git commit -m "Removed old file"
Keep the local file but remove it from Git
git rm --cached secrets.txt

Very useful when you accidentally commit:

.env
terraform.tfvars
config.json
10. git mv

Rename a file.

Instead of:

mv app.py main.py

Use:

git mv app.py main.py

Git tracks the rename properly.

11. git restore

Discard local changes.

git restore app.py

Returns the file to the last committed state.

⚠️ Local uncommitted changes will be lost.

Unstage a file
git restore --staged app.py

Removes it from the staging area while keeping your changes.

12. git clean

Remove untracked files.

Preview first:

git clean -n

Delete:

git clean -f

Useful after builds create temporary files.

13. git remote

View remotes:

git remote -v

Example:

origin
https://github.com/company/project.git

Add remote:

git remote add origin <repo-url>

Remove:

git remote remove origin
14. git fetch

Downloads remote changes.

git fetch

Updates remote-tracking branches only.

No merge.

15. git pull
git pull

Equivalent to:

git fetch
git merge

Downloads and integrates remote changes.

16. git push

Uploads commits.

git push origin main

First push:

git push -u origin main

The -u option sets the upstream branch.

